I must say, of all the things you did to make this role difficult,
the hunger is the most dastardly.  It creates, in the very early
game (like, dungeon levels one through three or so), an evil time
pressure that causes you to have to skip doing things that might
otherwise increase your chances of survival, because the time they
take would cause your tombstone to read "killed by a grid bug while
fainted from hunger".  Things other low-hitpoint roles (e.g., a
Tourist) would rely on being able to do to preserve his life, like
pet-testing every piece of armor and every weapon, are things you
don't have time to do as a convict, because you have no food and
are weak from hunger and dare not try to pray.  When you've lost
more than half your hitpoints (e.g., because you tried to go down
a flight of stairs while carrying an iron ball), you cannot afford
to hang out in a (relatively) safe corner and wait until you get
some of them back, because you'll starve.  You have to immediately
go in search of a monster to fight, in hopes of getting some food.
You have to go do that right away, even though you only have 5
hitpoints left and cannot pray, because otherwise you'll starve.

Compared to the hunger pressure, the striped shirt is no problem
at all.  You don't even have time to think about it.  I mean,
you wouldn't have time to mess around in a shop anyway, unless
it's got food.

